# 2026-08-02 (re-baseline): cshims.c hardening + multi_check documentation.
#
# TWO guest changes, batched deliberately into one re-baseline because the board reset costs the same
# whether one thing changes or five — and because NEITHER was worth this cost alone.
#
# 1. prover/methods/guest/cshims.c — the freestanding libc shims (hazync#55, external review).
#    `_sbrk` checked only its UPPER bound. `_malloc_trim_r` calls it with NEGATIVE increments to
#    release top-of-heap memory — confirmed by resolving the call sites in the linked guest ELF
#    (`neg a1,s0` immediately before `jal <_sbrk_r>`), not inferred from the function name — and for
#    incr < 0 the guard was trivially false, so the break moved unchecked and could in principle walk
#    below `_heap`. Latent only because newlib never released more than it took, which is a property
#    of the caller and not of the function.
#    `strtoul` ignored its `base` argument entirely: always decimal, no whitespace/sign/0x handling,
#    no overflow detection. All four call sites are libstdc++/newlib scaffolding (`__strftime`,
#    `_tzset_unlocked_r`, `std::random_device::_M_init_pretr1`, static init) — none on the consensus
#    path, and `_tzset` parses TZ, which `getenv` returns NULL for. Now standard C99.
#    Neither could produce a false proof. Heap exhaustion remains fail-closed: 1 MiB heap,
#    -fexceptions, and no `catch` anywhere in the guest validator, so `bad_alloc` reaches
#    std::terminate and no receipt is produced.
#
# 2. prover/methods/guest/src/main.rs — `multi_check` documented as the debug exerciser it is
